{
 "_note": "The sign system of the database. A grapheme is a letter of the script as a reader identifies it: its sign value (as RUNES-SPEC.md Appendix B and the engine's reader give it), its Turkological transliteration symbol, the phonemes it writes and, for the forms table of the engine's Eski-Teŋir-Too preset, the engine letter it stands for (engineKey). A glyph is one shape of a grapheme: each Unicode Old Turkic character is a glyph, and a shape Unicode does not encode (a turned or simplified form seen on a stone) is added as a glyph with unicode null, 'nearest' naming the character it is written with, and a description. Transcriptions cite glyph ids (b1.2) or, when the shape cannot be told, grapheme ids (b1).",
